# 4. THE AUDIO ENGINE — THE MOST IMPORTANT PART

Implement a real working audio engine using the Web Audio API.

Users must be able to:

1. Upload one or multiple local audio files.
2. Drag and drop audio files into the application.
3. Add audio using a clearly visible file-selection button.
4. Play and pause music.
5. Skip to the previous or next track.
6. Seek to any position using a timeline.
7. Adjust volume.
8. Mute and unmute.
9. Enable shuffle.
10. Enable repeat-one and repeat-all modes.
11. View the current track title and artist when available.
12. View the track duration and current playback time.
13. See album artwork when embedded artwork can be extracted reliably.
14. Build a queue or playlist from uploaded tracks.
15. Remove tracks from the queue.
16. See a clear empty state before loading music.

Support common browser-playable audio formats, including MP3, WAV, OGG, AAC/M4A where supported by the browser, and FLAC where supported.

Extract available metadata from local files when practical. If metadata is unavailable, use a sensible fallback title derived from the filename.

Never pretend that metadata exists when it does not.

Use object URLs or another appropriate browser-local mechanism to play files. Revoke object URLs when they are no longer needed.

Handle unsupported files, corrupted files, empty playlists, playback errors, and interrupted playback gracefully.

Do not attempt to bypass browser autoplay restrictions. Start playback in response to a user gesture when necessary.

## REAL-TIME ANALYSIS

Connect the playing audio to an AnalyserNode.

Use actual frequency-domain and time-domain data to drive the visualizations.

Analyze useful information such as:
- Frequency spectrum.
- Low-frequency energy.
- Mid-frequency energy.
- High-frequency energy.
- Waveform amplitude.
- Overall signal energy.
- Transient changes in the signal.
- Smoothed audio-reactive intensity.

Use sensible smoothing and interpolation to prevent jittery visuals.

Make bass-heavy sections produce convincing low-frequency movement, while higher-frequency content drives finer visual details.

Do not generate random movement and label it as audio-reactive.

The visuals must respond to the real audio currently playing.

If audio analysis becomes unavailable or the browser suspends the audio context, handle recovery cleanly.

IMPORTANT: Prevent audio duplication, feedback loops, and unnecessary AudioContext creation. Ensure the master playback volume works correctly and visualizations do not accidentally route audio through multiple output paths.

